WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.923
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:07.978
Jesus was born in a manger, and
Silicon Valley was born in a garage.

00:00:07.978 --> 00:00:13.515
With the venture capital
industry as its father and

00:00:13.515 --> 00:00:17.514
Stanford University as its mother.

00:00:17.514 --> 00:00:22.783
This region once home to plum orchards and
cattle ranches became the fertile

00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:27.248
ground for the world's greatest
technological innovations.

00:00:27.248 --> 00:00:33.264
Throughout the 20th century, the Legend of
Silicon Valley grew, attracting apostles,

00:00:33.264 --> 00:00:37.830
and engineers from all across the land
to join in the great salvation.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:42.289
That would be brought on by computers and
the Internet.

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:45.789
That is until tragically in 2001,

00:00:45.789 --> 00:00:51.951
irrational exuberance condemned
the Silicon Valley bubble to pop.

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.474
For three years, the valley laid dormant.

00:00:54.474 --> 00:00:58.273
Trillions were lost, or so it seemed.

00:00:58.273 --> 00:01:02.762
Until in 2004,
Silicon Valley miraculously rose again.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:06.999
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; With the IPO of Google incorporated.

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:11.491
Thus is the origin story for
Silicon Valley.

00:01:11.491 --> 00:01:17.439
The modern messiah of the world's
most powerful religion, capitalism.

00:01:19.761 --> 00:01:21.615
Every culture has it's own mythology.

00:01:21.615 --> 00:01:25.748
The fundamentally normative stories,
the preserve and

00:01:25.748 --> 00:01:29.805
perpetuate the values that
define the culture itself.

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:32.506
And Silicon valley is no different.

00:01:32.506 --> 00:01:37.540
We have our god-like founders, our
visionary prophets, our epic disruptors.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.880
Hewlett and Packard, building their first
audio oscillator in a Palo Alto garage.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.483
Bill Gates selling DOS to IBM for
retaining the licensing rights.

00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:51.565
Mark Zuckerberg, bringing his
band of misfits to the valley and

00:01:51.565 --> 00:01:53.690
hacking together the Facebook.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.199
But these myths have consequences.

00:01:57.519 --> 00:02:00.729
The myths of Silicon Valley,
and more specifically,

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:03.186
the archetypes who inhabit these myths.

00:02:03.186 --> 00:02:08.566
Form a sort of magnetic field that tugs
on the tech industry's moral compass,

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:12.246
shaping where we work,
what we build and how we lead.

00:02:12.246 --> 00:02:17.245
So I want to critically examine the nature
and the influence of three of these

00:02:17.245 --> 00:02:22.894
powerful Silicon Valley archetypes,
the founder, the oracle and the disruptor.

00:02:22.894 --> 00:02:27.902
Our first Silicon Valley
archetype is the hero himself,

00:02:27.902 --> 00:02:33.337
the technological Uber match,
the Superman, the founder.

00:02:33.337 --> 00:02:34.995
Who is this mythical founder?

00:02:34.995 --> 00:02:39.477
Well, they start their
career as a college dropout.

00:02:39.477 --> 00:02:43.511
Famed venture capitalists,
Peter Teal was giving high school students

00:02:43.511 --> 00:02:48.551
$100,000 grants to start companies,
instead of wasting their time in college.

00:02:48.551 --> 00:02:51.650
The founder is ruthlessly efficient.

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.441
They don't have time to eat meals,
so they drink Soylent.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.939
They can't waste precious metal cycles
trying to figure out what to wear every

00:02:58.939 --> 00:03:01.652
day, so they were the same t-shirt and
hoodie, and jeans.

00:03:01.652 --> 00:03:05.379
And the founder must be ruthlessly
efficient because they're

00:03:05.379 --> 00:03:09.328
frequently the CEO of two or three,
or four different companies.

00:03:09.328 --> 00:03:15.360
The soul and lifeblood of their respective
organizations, utterly irreplaceable.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.437
But I ask you to consider,

00:03:17.437 --> 00:03:22.267
what kind of future does
the Silicon Valley founder create?

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:24.229
For the founder, there's no room for
empathy, only efficiency.

00:03:24.229 --> 00:03:29.366
There are no people problems,
only engineering problems.

00:03:29.366 --> 00:03:33.048
Everything has an optimal,
measurable solution.

00:03:33.048 --> 00:03:36.983
And so our technology companies end up
building products that optimize for

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:37.739
engagement.

00:03:37.739 --> 00:03:41.794
With notifications and pings vying for
every second of our waking attention.

00:03:41.794 --> 00:03:43.614
Colors tuned to make us click,

00:03:43.614 --> 00:03:47.744
positive feedback loops of likes
triggering our dopamine response,

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:52.580
like slot machines, and cigarettes,
to have us tap again and again, and again.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:56.540
And so we end up with social networks that
leave us not connected, but depressed.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.623
With news that leaves us not informed,
but angry.

00:04:00.623 --> 00:04:06.014
With mobile games that leave us
not entertained, but addicted.

00:04:06.014 --> 00:04:10.600
There are only two types of
pushers of products that call

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:14.902
their customers users,
techies and drug dealers.

00:04:16.751 --> 00:04:20.839
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Our second Silicon Valley archetype is

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:21.621
the oracle.

00:04:21.621 --> 00:04:26.498
The oracle is the future seer, the thought
leader, the visionary who can predict

00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:30.117
the technological forces that
will shape our future world.

00:04:30.117 --> 00:04:35.075
Perhaps the most recent famous Silicon
Valley prophecy comes from the gospel of

00:04:35.075 --> 00:04:36.269
Marc Andreessen.

00:04:36.269 --> 00:04:40.952
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the Wall Street Journal in 2011,

00:04:40.952 --> 00:04:42.017
the Netscape founder and

00:04:42.017 --> 00:04:44.663
venture capitalist wrote that
software is eating the world.

00:04:44.663 --> 00:04:49.297
And it's not just individuals who
serve as the Silicon Valley oracle.

00:04:49.297 --> 00:04:53.813
The valley as a whole perpetuates
this sort of predictive zeitgeist for

00:04:53.813 --> 00:04:56.530
what will obviously be the next big thing.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:02.326
Machine learning, self-driving cars,
cryptocurrency, drones, chatbots.

00:05:02.326 --> 00:05:07.082
And so the rest of the world looks
to the Silicon Valley meta oracle to

00:05:07.082 --> 00:05:10.513
see where to invest or
where to brace for impact.

00:05:10.513 --> 00:05:16.543
But again, what kind of future does
this Silicon Valley oracle create?

00:05:16.543 --> 00:05:21.484
So much of Silicon Valley prediction is
predicated on this notion of technological

00:05:21.484 --> 00:05:22.550
inevitability.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:27.827
That the inexorable march of progress is
an unstoppable force of the universe.

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:32.134
And your only options as an individual are
to either invest or get out of the way.

00:05:34.393 --> 00:05:37.664
Automation is going to take your job so
you better learn to code.

00:05:37.664 --> 00:05:40.117
Your digital footprint is everywhere so

00:05:40.117 --> 00:05:43.153
you better reconceive of
your notion of privacy.

00:05:43.153 --> 00:05:47.410
Electric scooter is the next
big advance in mobility, so

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.169
you better hop on or get left behind.

00:05:50.169 --> 00:05:54.775
But the silicon valley oracle believes
in these things coming true, and

00:05:54.775 --> 00:05:57.053
they very match well may come true.

00:05:57.053 --> 00:06:02.766
But it's the attitude of the oracle of
inevitability that removes human agency,

00:06:02.766 --> 00:06:07.426
and that's human responsibility
from the future that we create.

00:06:07.426 --> 00:06:10.133
It is not a human hands so
this logic goes but

00:06:10.133 --> 00:06:14.211
the invisible hand on the wheel
that stirs the ship of progress.

00:06:14.211 --> 00:06:18.811
Who will be the winners in this
supposedly inevitable future?

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:19.682
And who will be left behind?

00:06:19.682 --> 00:06:23.588
Our third and final Silicon Valley
archetype is the disruptor.

00:06:23.588 --> 00:06:28.426
Silicon Valley is obsessed with
disruption of innovative new

00:06:28.426 --> 00:06:34.017
technologies coming in and
turning existing industries upside down.

00:06:34.017 --> 00:06:37.335
The pre-eminent global startup conference
is called Tech Crunch Disrupt.

00:06:37.335 --> 00:06:41.493
There are firms dedicated
to disruption consulting.

00:06:41.493 --> 00:06:45.446
At USC today you can get a degree
in disruptive innovation.

00:06:45.446 --> 00:06:48.499
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Perhaps the God-king of recent

00:06:48.499 --> 00:06:50.570
Silicon Valley disruption is Uber.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:55.519
The taxi industry in its antediluvian
period is rife with inefficiency.

00:06:55.519 --> 00:06:59.340
In comes Uber, you can summon
a car at the touch of a button,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.325
the status quo is offended and
the disruptor reigns supreme.

00:07:03.325 --> 00:07:08.222
But what kind of future does
the Silicon Valley disruptor create?

00:07:08.222 --> 00:07:13.740
The disruptor in these myths is the
deliverer of a sort of Darwinian justice.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.762
But the old order content and
its dominance, and

00:07:17.762 --> 00:07:21.013
stagnation deserves to be taken down.

00:07:21.013 --> 00:07:23.685
And so
the disruptor in these myths doesn't, and

00:07:23.685 --> 00:07:28.081
indeed mustn't think too hard about
the collateral damage of their disruption.

00:07:28.081 --> 00:07:32.932
Yeah, what Silicon Valley companies
are disrupting today is no longer just

00:07:32.932 --> 00:07:34.079
old hard drives.

00:07:34.079 --> 00:07:37.820
It's people, it's labor markets,
it's eduction, it's healthcare.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.681
And today's Silicon Valley
disruptor is looking less and

00:07:41.681 --> 00:07:45.551
less like the creator of
a fundamentally new technology.

00:07:45.551 --> 00:07:47.898
And more, and more like an adept
user of venture capital dollars.

00:07:47.898 --> 00:07:51.668
To undercut prices and dodge regulation,
and avoid labor laws, and

00:07:51.668 --> 00:07:56.153
exploit public places, and under pay
workers, and strong-arm suppliers, and

00:07:56.153 --> 00:07:57.261
achieve monopoly.

00:07:57.261 --> 00:08:02.394
What Silicon Valley so
mythologizes as disruptive innovation

00:08:02.394 --> 00:08:06.765
is robber baron capitalism
reborn in the mobile era.

00:08:09.973 --> 00:08:15.121
These three archetypes, the founder,
the oracle, and the disruptor formed

00:08:15.121 --> 00:08:20.896
the foundations of today's Silicon Valley
institutions, and culture, and values.

00:08:20.896 --> 00:08:23.957
But these myths are trapped
in a different era.

00:08:23.957 --> 00:08:26.721
They're trapped in an era of silicon,

00:08:26.721 --> 00:08:31.927
they're trapped in an era of wafers and
chips of servers and cellphones.

00:08:31.927 --> 00:08:33.297
Our world has changed.

00:08:33.297 --> 00:08:38.969
The problems that Silicon Valley tackles
today are of a much more human form,

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:44.831
of relationships, of community,
of biology, of the nature of truth itself.

00:08:44.831 --> 00:08:48.606
Our old myths are not fit for
this new era.

00:08:48.606 --> 00:08:53.776
So who is it that we want to be doing
the creating that we want to deliver

00:08:53.776 --> 00:08:58.338
us into a future built for
everyone and not just a select few.

00:08:58.338 --> 00:09:03.511
I want the humanist who focuses
not on achieving growth,

00:09:03.511 --> 00:09:06.761
but on achieving sustainability.

00:09:06.761 --> 00:09:10.872
Who doesn't just optimize for
engagement, but optimizes for empathy.

00:09:10.872 --> 00:09:16.164
For whom diversity and inclusion don't
just make their company stronger,

00:09:16.164 --> 00:09:18.154
but make society stronger.

00:09:18.154 --> 00:09:22.590
I want the artist who doesn't just
see technology as instrumental and

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.835
commercial, but
as inspirational and creative.

00:09:25.835 --> 00:09:31.123
Who doesn't just look for solutions that
are optimal, but that are beautiful.

00:09:31.123 --> 00:09:35.018
For whom progress means not
achieving greater returns, but

00:09:35.018 --> 00:09:37.853
achieving greater global understanding.

00:09:37.853 --> 00:09:43.115
And I want the synthesizer who doesn't
just look to technology as the solution

00:09:43.115 --> 00:09:48.155
to every problem, but to community,
or to government, or to education.

00:09:48.155 --> 00:09:53.092
For whom technology is not an end in and
of itself, but just one of many

00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:58.136
different means for fulfilling our
humanities greatest potential.

00:09:58.136 --> 00:10:02.461
Silicon Valley will always represent
a great force for a change.

00:10:02.461 --> 00:10:08.034
But I believe that all of us in the Valley
are fundamentally responsible for

00:10:08.034 --> 00:10:10.253
the shape that change takes.

00:10:10.253 --> 00:10:13.054
But Silicon Valley needs new myths.

00:10:13.054 --> 00:10:15.419
We need new heroes.

00:10:15.419 --> 00:10:18.128
And I believe that those
heroes could be all of you.

00:10:18.128 --> 00:10:18.740
Thank you.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.197
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:21.197 --> 00:10:26.288
[MUSIC]

